       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. 4,250 mg three times daily comes to 12,750 mg/day, which is about five times the usual maximum for metformin. The FDA-labeled maximum is 2,550 mg/day, and some references allow up to 3,000 mg/day. A single 4,250 mg dose is also far above any standard dose. At this level the risk of metformin-associated lactic acidosis is serious, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nDid you mean one of these?\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals** (2,550 mg/day, the maximum labeled dose)\n- **425 mg PO three times daily** (this isn't a standard tablet strength, so it's less likely)\n- **1,000 mg PO twice daily** (2,000 mg/day)\n- Another dose\n\nIf 4,250 mg is what you intend, tell me and include the clinical rationale. I'd want that confirmed before entering it, and it may also trigger pharmacy review. Otherwise, tell me the corrected dose and I'll enter it right away."
      }
